# Shell scripts and job files must be LF on every platform.
#
# On Windows, git checks these out with CRLF. Git Bash tolerates a CRLF
# script -- and, worse, translates \r away on read, so `grep` and `bash -n`
# there both report a clean file. WSL bash does not tolerate it:
# `set -Eeuo pipefail\r` fails with "pipefail: invalid option name", and a
# heredoc terminator followed by \r is never recognised, so the file dies
# with "unexpected end of file".
#
# That combination makes the failure invisible from the shell most likely to
# be used to check it. It matters here because the FarmShare launchers are
# run from WSL -- the SSH control socket lives there -- while git runs on the
# Windows side. sync_repo.sh strips \r on the *remote* copy, which kept the
# problem hidden until the laptop-side scripts were run directly.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sbatch text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf

# Python under .edullm carries shebangs and is executed directly on
# FarmShare; `#!/usr/bin/env python3\r` is not a valid interpreter line.
# Scoped to this directory to avoid renormalising the upstream tree.
.edullm/**/*.py text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one thing that must stay CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Leave upstream data files exactly as committed; they are not scripts and
# renormalising them would produce a five-thousand-line diff for no reason.
*.csv -text
*.txt -text
